<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="UTP and CTP de novo biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7176" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Pyrimidine and purine nucleotides are found in all living organisms. They are essential components of nucleic acids, and play a crucial role in growth, development and reproduction, as well as in a large number of essential metabolic progressions such as interconversions from sugars to polysaccharides, glycoproteins and phospholipids |CITS: [9576794][12369616][16024685][16669783]|. The &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; pyrimidine nucleotide biosynthetic pathway (see |FRAME: PWY-5686|) converts |FRAME: HCO3|, |FRAME: GLN|, |FRAME: L-ASPARTATE| and |FRAME: PRPP| (PRPP) to |FRAME: UMP| (UMP). This pathway appears to be one of the most ancient biochemical pathways as it is evolutionary conserved in all species. UMP is subsequently converted to other pyrimidines, as described in this pathway. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; biosynthesis of pyrimidine nucleotides starts with |FRAME: UMP|, and proceeds via |FRAME: UDP| to |FRAME: UTP|. |FRAME: CTP| is only formed &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; by deamination of |FRAME: UTP|. The first enzyme of the pathway, |FRAME: UMPKI-CPLX|, catalyzes the phosphorylation of UMP to form UDP. The eukaryotic enzyme also catalyzes the phosphorylation of CMP to CDP, and was thus named |FRAME: AT5G26667-MONOMER| |CITS: [15604701]|. The next enzyme in the pathway, |FRAME: NUCLEOSIDE-DIP-KIN-CPLX| (NDK), catalyzes a reaction in which the terminal phosphate of a nucleoside-triphosphate is transferred to a nucleoside-diphosphate - in this case, the transfer of a phosphate from the purine ATP to the pyrimidine UDP, forming UTP. The enzyme has a broad substrate specificity, and is involved in the biosynthesis of several nucleoside-triphosphates, including formation of CTP from CDP. In the plant |FRAME: TAX-3702| the enzyme is encoded by a small gene family and has been demonstrated to mediate polar auxin transport |CITS: [15927941]| and interact with other proteins conferring stress tolerance |CITS: [12506203]|. Interconversion between thymidine and cytidine nucleotides is made possible by the glutamine (or ammonia)-dependent enzyme |FRAME: CTPSYN-CPLX|.</Description>
